\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces How consensus scoring works. (a)\nobreakspace  {}A sentence is translated by $K=4$ LLM families; families A--C produce logically equivalent formulas (up to vocabulary), while family\nobreakspace  {}D swaps the condition and conclusion. (b)\nobreakspace  {}Candidate from family\nobreakspace  {}A: vocabulary-aligned z3 equivalence with B, C succeeds () and fails with D (\texttimes ), giving a consensus score of $1/3 = 0.33$ (low, likely correct). (c)\nobreakspace  {}Candidate from family\nobreakspace  {}D: equivalence fails with all three peers, giving a score of $3/3 = 1.0$ (high, likely erroneous).}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:method_diagram}{{2}{4}{How consensus scoring works. (a)~A sentence is translated by $K=4$ LLM families; families A--C produce logically equivalent formulas (up to vocabulary), while family~D swaps the condition and conclusion. (b)~Candidate from family~A: vocabulary-aligned z3 equivalence with B, C succeeds (\checkmark ) and fails with D (\texttimes ), giving a consensus score of $1/3 = 0.33$ (low, likely correct). (c)~Candidate from family~D: equivalence fails with all three peers, giving a score of $3/3 = 1.0$ (high, likely erroneous)}{figure.2}{}}
